%I A398820 #8 Aug 12 2026 01:36:56
%S A398820 4,9,12,16,18,20,25,28,36,44,45,48,49,50,52,60,63,64,68,75,76,80,81,
%T A398820 84,90,92,98,99,100,112,116,117,121,124,126,132,140,144,147,148,150,
%U A398820 153,156,162,164,169,171,172,175,176,180,188,192,196,198,204,207,208,212,220
%N A398820 Numbers whose powerful part (A057521) is a square that is larger than 1.
%C A398820 Subsequence of A368714 and differs from it by not having the terms 1, 432, 648, 1728, 2000, ... .
%C A398820 Subsequence of A240112 and differs from it by not having the terms 108, 288, 432, 500, 540, ... .
%C A398820 Differs from its subsequence A384519 by having the terms 144, 324, 400, 576, 720, 784, ... .
%C A398820 Nonsquarefree numbers whose prime factorization contains exponents that are either 1 or even.
%C A398820 Each term in this sequence has a unique representation as the product of two coprime numbers: a squarefree number and a square that is larger than 1.
%C A398820 The asymptotic density of this sequence is Product_{p prime} (1 - 1/(p^2*(p+1))) - 1/zeta(2) = A065465 - A059956 = 0.273586737871144148265... .
%H A398820 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398820/b398820.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398820 <a href="/index/Eu#epf">Index entries for sequences computed from exponents in factorization of n</a>.
%e A398820 12 = 2^2 * 3 is a term since its powerful part, 4 = 2^2, is a square that is larger than 1.
%t A398820 q[k_] := Module[{e = FactorInteger[k][[;; , 2]]}, AnyTrue[e, # > 1 &] && AllTrue[e, # == 1 || EvenQ[#] &]]; Select[Range[220], q]
%o A398820 (PARI) isok(k) = if(k == 1, 0, my(e = factor(k)[, 2]); vecmax(e) > 1 && #select(x -> (x > 1 && x%2), e) == 0);
%Y A398820 Intersection of A013929 and A335275.
%Y A398820 A335275 is a disjoint union of A005117 and this sequence.
%Y A398820 Subsequence of A240112 and A368714.
%Y A398820 Subsequences: A384519, A398821.
%Y A398820 Cf. A000290, A013661, A057521, A059956, A065465.
%K A398820 nonn,easy,new
%O A398820 1,1
